<BaseLayout
  title="Function vectors | Devansh Ruia"
  description="A method note on extracting and testing function vectors, with the case viewer held pending provenance validation."
>
  <h1>Function vectors</h1>
  <p class="muted">
    A function vector is an activation-space intervention tested for whether it can shift a model
    toward a particular behavior. The useful claim is causal and setup-specific, not that the vector
    reveals a clean internal category.
  </p>

  <h2>Method</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Define demonstrations and evaluation probes before inspecting outputs.</li>
    <li>Trace the model activations at a named model, layer, and location.</li>
    <li>Select and combine task-relevant contributions under a documented rule.</li>
    <li>Inject the resulting vector at the specified intervention location.</li>
    <li>Compare steered and baseline outputs with a stated scoring rule, split, and seed.</li>
  </ol>

  <aside class="notice">
    <h2>Viewer held for validation</h2>
    <p>
      The stored country-to-capital, antonym, and present-to-past cases are not public here because
      their model, layer, head source, evaluation split or seed, scoring rule, and run artifact are
      not recorded together. The antonym score and present-to-past note therefore cannot be traced
      to a repository artifact.
    </p>
    <p>
      A tool-selection case is also withheld because no case record or activation-intervention
      artifact exists. The viewer will return when each case has enough provenance to reproduce and
      audit its result.
    </p>
  </aside>
</BaseLayout>
